@401 = ~Almion's Scepter of Power~
@402 = ~Almion's Scepter of Power is, without the slightest doubt, one of the most coveted artifacts on the Dragon Coast. According to legend, the evil archmage Almion Celmo created this scepter to offer it to a mighty warrior in exchange for a single promise: the warrior was to play a role in the assassination of the king of a distant realm, seize the throne, and later surrender his newfound power to the archmage. But the plan failed, and the archmage ultimately ended up imprisoned.

Some whisper that Almion placed a curse upon the scepter, which would explain why it always vanishes sooner or later. It is said that the artifact refuses to serve anyone but its creator.

STATISTICS:

Allows wielder to cast High-Level Mage spells as a 20th-level caster, with a casting time of 1, three times per day for each spell level. Choose from:

Level 7: Mass Invisibility, Mordenkainen's Sword, Improved Mantle
Level 8: Abi-Dalzim's Horrid Wilting, Simulacrum, Keleg-Ze's Vengeful Bolts
Level 9: Shapechange, Black Blade of Disaster, Time Stop~

@403 = ~Marvelous Shoes~
@404 = ~According to Cormyrean folklore, Viettro was a young tailor from a poor village who dreamed of attending the Magivérone Ball at the royal palace. Though he had fashioned himself a handsome set of clothes, he possessed neither shoes, nor an invitation from the royal family, nor any knowledge of courtly etiquette.

One day, a kindly wizard heard his wish and presented him with a pair of enchanted shoes. They were elegant enough for the ball, and the wizard promised that they would provide Viettro with whatever he should need when the time came.

Taking the wizard at his word, Viettro arrived at the palace gates on the day of the ball, clad in his finest garments and wearing his new shoes. The guards on duty demanded to see his invitation, and suddenly the young man felt a weight in his pocket. Reaching inside, he found a flask of fine liqueur, which he offered to the guards in exchange for passage.

Throughout the ball, he found himself in conversation with viscounts and marquises. Viettro was certain that his ignorance would betray him, yet he spoke with such profound knowledge of history and courtly custom that his companions were greatly impressed.

His faith in the wizard's shoes continued to grow, and when he beheld the most beautiful woman at the ball, he asked her for a dance. The shoes guided both his steps and his words, and his charm was so great that before the year's end the young man and the princess were wed.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:

All Attributes: +1
Armor Class: +2
THAC0: +2
Saving Throws: +2
Luck: +2
Thieving Skills: +15%
Magic Resistance: +15%
Elemental Resistance: +15%
Magical Damage Resistance: +15%
Physical Damage Resistance: +15%
One Additional 5th-Level Wizard Spell
One Additional 4th-Level Priest Spell~

@405 = ~Malloy's Fortunate Ring~
@406 = ~This ring was among the most prized possessions of Mikael Malloy, a halfling mercenary from Chessenta. He was a solitary fellow who preferred to work alone whenever possible. His countrymen seldom objected. Few could endure Malloy's company for long. Nearly everyone found him rude, cruel and, in general, thoroughly disagreeable, even among the ruthless Renegades.

Malloy never tired of boasting about the countless foes he had defeated, the great beasts he had slain, and all those he had crushed beneath his boot. To hear him tell it, even the gods trembled at the sound of his name. Though few, if any, believed his outlandish tales, none could deny that the halfling was one hard customer who had survived many lesser conflicts by his own hand.

Upon his retirement, Malloy settled in the Vast to live a quiet and comfortable life. But his many rivals and enemies had not forgotten him. Barely a few weeks after retiring, his body was found in the privy. His neighbors, unsurprised by the discovery, informed the magistrate that Malloy must have accidentally cut off his own head while shaving. Nothing had been taken save the ring.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
May Use Any Item (provided attribute requirements are met)
All Attributes: +3
Special: A wizard wearing armor while equipped with this ring retains the ability to cast spells.~

@407 = ~Munimentum~
@408 = ~During the Age of the Elder Kings, the dwarf-king Tangrim summoned the royal smith Armandur to his court to discuss the forging of a royal signet ring.

"My heirs shall wear this ring for centuries to come," the aged king explained. "The crown you forged for me shall command them respect, and your Sceptre shall grant them authority. But just as I have made enemies, so too shall they, and they cannot wear the royal armor every hour of every day. Use your craft once more in service to the Royal House of Tangrim. Forge for me and my family a means to protect ourselves from our foes, by day and by night, from this day until the dark hour when the last dwarf falls."
Armandur returned to his forge and pondered the king's request for a long while. It seemed impossible. His skill was legendary, but he was only a goldsmith, not a wizard. The crown and the Sceptre he had crafted for the king were but symbols; they possessed no power save the faith that others placed in them. Fearing that King Tangrim would cast him into the royal dungeon if he failed, Armandur went to Moradin's great temple and prayed for guidance.
Armandur prayed throughout the night, not merely for his own safety, but for the welfare of his family, who would be left destitute should he fail to satisfy the king. When he returned home the next morning, he was a changed dwarf. His eyes burned with an almost fanatical fervor, and he scarcely spoke to his wife and children save to tell them not to disturb him.
He went straight into his foundry and barred the door behind him, refusing food, drink, and even conversation. For the rest of that day and the following night, the only sounds to emerge from Armandur's workshop were the ringing of his tools and the hiss of molten gold upon the forge. Fearing that madness had overtaken him, his wife never dared open the foundry door. Instead, she fell asleep outside, pleading with Armandur to rest.
When Armandur's wife awoke, the foundry door stood open, and Armandur was blinking in the light of dawn.
"Are you ill, my wife?" he asked. "Why are you sleeping outside my foundry?"
"How did you open the door without waking me?" she replied. "I lay against it all night waiting for you to come out."
"Come out?" said Armandur, helping his wife to her feet. "I have only just returned from the temple, and found you here when I came to begin my work. You see, I was granted a vision of four rings that I am to forge for King Tangrim—one each for him, his wife, and his children—and I must begin at once. I shall need you to bring my meals here, if you please."
Armandur's wife could only stare in astonishment.
"But you have been working! All day and all night! Look there, upon your workbench—you have already forged the four rings!"
Armandur looked, and there upon the bench rested the very rings he had envisioned while praying in the temple, already completed and awaiting only presentation to the king. Before he could examine them further, King Tangrim himself appeared at the foundry door.
"The priest you sent to summon me tells me that you have completed the task I set before you," said Tangrim, as Armandur and his wife knelt before their sovereign. "Is it true? Have you forged four rings for me and my family?"
Armandur longed to inspect the rings before surrendering them to the king, for he could not fathom how they had been made while he himself had not been there to craft them. But the king caught the smith's quick glance toward the workbench and followed it to the rings.
"They are magnificent!" Tangrim declared. "Master Smith, you have surpassed yourself! As your reward, I grant you a wagon laden with gold, that neither you nor your descendants shall ever lack the materials to fashion your wonders."
Armandur thanked the king profusely, but when the gold arrived, his course was already decided. Closing his workshop and packing his belongings, he took his family and the wagon of gold as far from Tangrim's kingdom as he could travel. There he spent every last coin building a mighty temple to Moradin.
From that day forth, the High Priests and High Priestesses of that temple have all been descendants of Armandur, the goldsmith who prayed for a miracle—and received one.
Through the ages, the Rings of the Dwarf Kings protected the descendants of Tangrim, but one by one they fell into the hands of enemies and were destroyed. Now only Munimentum, the ring forged for Tangrim's son Tormund, remains. By all appearances it rests within the Temple of Moradin, awaiting the return of one of Tangrim's heirs to the throne of the dwarven realms.
Yet the High Priests have known for the last three centuries that the ring they guard so carefully is but a copy. The true Munimentum was lost during a great feast and believed destroyed. In truth, however, the fire was deliberately set by a thief to conceal both his escape and his crime.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Armor Class: +3
- +3 bonus to Saving Throws vs. Spell and Death
- Damage Resistance: +30%
- Effects are doubled when worn by a dwarf.~

@409 = ~Cloak of Semi-Reality~
@4010 = ~The fabric of this cloak appears to have been woven in a mystical pattern that shifts with the garment's every movement, causing the eye to perceive things that do not exist.
The students of Halruaa's Academy of Magic have long conducted discreet experiments into the nature of illusion, fascinated by theories claiming that the world around them is a deception, a dream, or perhaps both. One year, inspired by these notions, an entire class of ambitious apprentices formed a society of existential skeptics. They wore cloaks such as this one to recognize one another outside their secret gatherings.

Years later, a dozen of these cloaks were discovered buried beneath stacks of books in a library. Though the fate of their owners and the outcome of their research remain unknown, the cloaks have since passed from hand to hand as arcane curiosities.

Equipped Abilities: (Partial Incorporeality)

Armor Class: +3
Saving Throws: +2
Magic Resistance: +25%
Stealth: +40%
Immunity to Non-Magical and +1 Weapons

Charge Ability:

Once per day, the wearer may assume a state of partial incorporeality (Improved Invisibility and +25% Magic Resistance for 1 turn; this effect is not illusory).

Weight: 1~
@401019 = ~Improved Invisibility~

@4011 = ~Martyr's Boots~
@4012 = ~Ydwen was a paladin of the Order of the Shimmering Rose during the settlement of the Cold Vale. She was renowned for escorting settlers and farmers across the Cold Vale, protecting them from the Uthgardt barbarians. Though she was a seasoned warrior, she avoided fighting the Uthgardts whenever possible. As tensions rose and tempers flared, she once found herself leading a family of farmers away from the ruins of their home. While attempting to catch the barbarians by surprise, one of her companions charged ahead. Ydwen was forced to hold the Uthgardts at bay while the settlers escaped. Surrounded and overwhelmed, she fell in battle, and it is said that even the warrior who struck the killing blow wept for her.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
The wearer is continuously affected by the following priest spells:

Armor of Faith (15% resistance to physical and magical damage)
Draw Upon Holy Might (+3 Strength, Dexterity, and Constitution; does not stack)
Ilmater's Endurance (Hit Points doubled; +2 bonus to Saving Throws, +3 vs. Petrification/Polymorph; immunity to disease and wing buffet attacks)

Charge Abilities:

Cure Disease twice per day
Cure Moderate Wounds twice per day

Usable By:

Paladin
Any non-evil Cleric~

@4013 = ~Squirrelinator~
@4014 = ~This is, to say the least... a rather peculiar item. As far as anyone knows, it was crafted near the end of the First Bloom by Kyanka, the royal mage and enchanter, who had long since lost his wits. He was said to possess a decidedly unusual sense of humor, which may explain the perverse delight he took in robbing his foes of coherent thought before finishing them off. This robe appears to have seen very little use.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Ability:

Casts Polymorph Other upon any creature that attacks the wearer, with a -2 penalty to its Saving Throw.

Charge Abilities:

Three times per day: Polymorph Other with a -6 penalty to the target's Saving Throw.

Armor Class: 5
Weight: 6
Usable By:

Mage~

@4015 = ~Magicae Frativia Futa~
@4016 = ~"Magic has been corrupted.

The old way has been forsaken. The Nine Primordial Dweomers have been lost. The pure blood of the Ancients has been tainted.
We are fading away.
There was a time when wizards worshipped us as gods, but now they mock us and call us fools. They have stolen our magic and caged our minds. They have reduced our beautiful songs to meaningless babble. They have corrupted the language of our ancestors and imprisoned it within the pages of their precious grimoires.
Arcane magic is unnatural. It disrupts the Great Cycle of Magic. It destroys the harmony of the universe. Wizards are parasites; we are symbiotes. With every spell they cast, our power wanes and our numbers grow fewer. Their insatiable hunger for knowledge will bring about the ruin of the world. They must be stopped.
The rings you now bear were forged by our greatest artisans. They embody our bond with the spirit of magic and shall restore to the Great Cycle the magic that the wizards have stolen.
They shall make your powers great enough to strip the usurpers of their spells.
Today we march to war. Today we shall reclaim what was stolen from us. Today we shall prevail."

— From the address of Vorsam the Faithful to the Five Generals of the Eladrin Rebellion before the First Battle of the War of the Mages.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Unlimited Spell Turning
- Each successful attack against a wizard causes the target to forget one memorized spell
- If worn by an elf: +50% Magic Resistance (+25% if worn by a half-elf)
- If worn by a mage: +6 bonus to Saving Throws vs. Spell (+3 for multiclass mages, +2 for triple-class mages)

Charge Abilities:
- Magic Misdirection (arcane magic only, 100%, no Saving Throw) twice per day
- Faerie Globe of Invulnerability twice per day
- Magic Disjunction (100%) twice per day~
@40161 = ~Magic Disjunction~

@4017 = ~The Abjuration~
@4018 = ~Forged in ages long past, the Abjuration serves its purpose as faithfully today as it did when first wrought. Created to withstand the incursions of mighty wizards, the Abjuration has distinguished itself upon hundreds of battlefields and protected its bearers through countless wars. Violence follows the Abjuration wherever it goes, from mighty empires that covet the shield for their own campaigns to the mages who long to see the accursed thing destroyed. Throughout its long history, nothing has ever marred its surface, much less damaged it. Thus the Abjuration remains an indomitable bane to spellcasters across the world.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Armor Class: +5, +1 bonus vs. Missile Attacks
- Magic Resistance: +25%
- +4 bonus to Saving Throws vs. Spell

Charge Abilities:
- May cast Spell Shield and Spell Turning (40 spell levels) once per day each

Weight: 10
Required Strength: 10

Not Usable By:
Bard
Druid
Mage
Thief~
@40181 = ~Spell Shield~

@4019 = ~Vridomir, the Undead Scourge~
@4020 = ~Vridomir achieved little success as an Archmage. Afflicted with a crippling disease of the bones that slowly left him paralyzed, he was shunned by his peers and terrified of death. Desperate to prolong his life, he sought every means imaginable.
Vridomir tried everything. He performed vile rituals in the names of dark gods. He committed unspeakable atrocities against innocents to strengthen his own power. With no other path left to him, Vridomir sold his soul to infernal beings in exchange for a longer life.
Only then did he realize his mistake. The fiends had granted his wish, but not without a cruel sense of irony. The Archmage was kept alive within his mortal shell even as the disease continued to consume his bones. After a century, he had become little more than a half-living monstrosity, immortal yet hideously disfigured.
Vridomir lacked the talent to become a true lich, but that did not deter him. Combining his mastery of the arcane arts with his obsession for immortality, he transferred his essence into a magical staff through an arcane ritual of his own design.
This dreadful staff became Vridomir's vessel for eternal life.

Combat Abilities:

Instantly slays creatures of less than 8 Hit Dice and all summoned creatures on a successful hit
Paralyzes the target for 5 rounds on a successful hit (Save vs. Spell negates)
Drains one experience level on a successful hit (Save vs. Death negates)

Charge Ability:

Once per day, Vridomir's spirit (22nd-level Sorcerer) may emerge from the staff for 3 turns, assuming the form of a spectre to aid the party.

Equipped Abilities:

Immunity to Fear, Paralysis, and Level Drain.

THAC0: +5
Damage: 1D6 +5, +1D10 Cold Damage
Speed Factor: 0
Proficiency Type: Quarterstaff
Type: Two-Handed
Requires: 5 Strength

Weight: 8~
@40201 = ~Vridomir Level 1~
@40202 = ~Vridomir Level 2~
@40203 = ~Vridomir Level 3~
@40204 = ~Vridomir Level 4~
@40205 = ~Vridomir Level 5~
@40206 = ~Vridomir Level 6~
@40207 = ~Vridomir Level 7~
@40208 = ~Vridomir Level 8~
@40209 = ~Vridomir Level 9~
@402010 = ~Lich Touch~

@4021 = ~Vastanir~
@4022 = ~This blade seems to appear and vanish from history with nearly every generation. Countless nobles, warriors, and adventurers have wielded it and, empowered by its might, accomplished deeds both glorious and terrible. Their names are seldom remembered, as though they mattered less than the weapon itself. None of its owners kept the sword for long, for each inevitably met a violent end. It is said that the blade sings a strange song within the hearts of those who wield it, inspiring them to push themselves beyond their mortal limits—a gift that rarely ends well. Thus has the sword passed from hand to hand throughout the ages.
Most recently, it was recovered from the frozen corpse of a Deepwater explorer in the Silver Marches. It is said that he found the sword lodged within the ribcage of a dragon long dead, beside the rusted remains of a half-digested suit of armor. No one knows where or how the dragon's unfortunate victim first acquired the weapon, though it is reasonable to conclude that grim fortune played its part.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- +1 Strength, Constitution, and Dexterity
- Critical Hit Chance: +2%
- Improved Haste

Combat Ability:
- 10% chance on hit to reduce the wielder's Hit Points by 1D4+2 while increasing Strength, Dexterity, and Constitution by 1 for 1 turn.

Damage: 1D8 +5
THAC0: +5
Damage Type: Slashing
Weight: 3
Speed Factor: 1
Proficiency Type: Long Sword
Type: One-Handed
Required Strength: 6

Not Usable By:
 Druid
 Cleric
 Mage~

@4023 = ~Hood of Protection~
@4024 = ~These simple, dark-colored hoods create an illusion of greater power. They are enchanted with dweomers that allow the wearer to benefit from the powers of multiple magical rings at once. Such rare items are difficult to craft and are usually found in the possession of powerful priests and wizards. They cannot be fashioned to suit any particular alignment or moral creed.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Free Action
- Chaotic Commands
- Reflects spells of levels 1 through 4~

@4025 = ~<CHARNAME>'s Golden Heart~
@4026 = ~You have no idea where this dwarven-forged sword came from, but you can clearly make out the name <CHARNAME> etched upon its blade. You are certain you have never seen it before, yet it feels strangely familiar in your hands. It appears quite ancient, its edge chipped in several places, and a faint chill radiates from the steel. You cannot help but wonder why this sword bears your name.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- +2 Strength and Dexterity
- +25 Maximum Hit Points
- Free Action
- Haste

THAC0: +4
Damage: 1D12 +4
Damage Type: Slashing
Weight: 10
Speed Factor: 6
Proficiency Type: Two-Handed Sword
Type: Two-Handed
Required Strength: 14

Not Usable By:
 Druid
 Cleric
 Mage
 Thief~

@4027 = ~Ring of Supreme Luck~
@4028 = ~"'The lucky ring?' asked the old farmer—rhetorically, since I had just put that very question to him. He stroked a beard that might once have been snow-white, had it not accumulated decades of grime. His gaunt frame looked ready to surrender at any moment and collapse into a tangled heap of bones. All the while he rocked, ever so slowly, back and forth upon the creaking porch of his cottage, pondering my simple question.
"'Ayup, I reckon I know a tale or two about it,' he finally muttered, the words escaping his mouth at much the same pace as his rocking chair.
"'Well then, perhaps you'd be willing to share one of those tales with me?' I replied, trying not to sound impatient. I had spent far too much time in taverns I would normally have avoided, all in pursuit of rumors concerning this ring.
"He looked at me with eyes surprisingly dark, and for a fleeting moment I wondered whether I had unknowingly offended a wizard in disguise. The moment passed, replaced by the distant stare of a man lost in memory. He rocked a little longer before speaking again.
"'That lucky ring ain't no ordinary magical trinket, no sir. There's something to it. Best thing you could ever do for yourself is find it and hang onto it for as long as you can. That ring there is pure power.'
"My heart leapt. This was precisely the sort of information I had been seeking.
"The old man continued. 'Now, nobody rightly knows where the ring came from, but the first time I ever heard tell of it was when old Touker found it while plowing his cornfield. He dug up the ring and figured he'd take it down to the tavern and wager it in a card game. By the end of the week he had so much platinum he didn't know what to do with it.'
"'So the ring worked? It really brought him luck?' I asked.
"'Sure did,' the old man rasped. 'Then, after he got himself killed in that out-of-season hurricane, his widow started wearing it. She won just about everything she set her hand to... right up until the griffons ate her.'
"'And adventurers? Warriors, or wizards like myself? Have you heard tales of any of them using the ring?'
"I hoped that by steering him in the right direction he might answer the question I truly cared about: would this ring help me defeat the Dark Lord Lorquatz?
"'Oh, plenty. Widow Touker's niece got the ring next. After the tool shed fell on her, a warrior took it—you know the sort, called himself Red-Eyed Pete. Wore the ring in every battle. Swore it made him fight better, and by the gods, you'd have believed him if you'd seen him. Every swing of his axe struck true. Shame about that potion, though. If somebody's going to put green slime in a potion bottle, they ought to label it properly.'
"'After Pete's flesh melted away, a young sorceress named Lyria came into possession of the ring. Said it strengthened her spellcasting. Then a pack of enraged quaggoths stripped her to the bone, and the rogue Dirian found it. They never discovered enough of him to tell how he died, but the next owner, a bard, suddenly burst into flames one day. Burned alive in front of the whole town! Strangest thing. Then there was the monk who choked on a swarm of locusts, the barbarian who fell down a flight of stairs and broke his neck, the psion who got himself impaled on a statue, the paladin trampled beneath a stampede of wildebeests, and the...'
"'Wait, wait!' I cried. 'Has anyone ever worn this ring without dying some sudden and utterly improbable death?'
"'Why, sure they have, son!' the old man chuckled. 'I'm certain of it—I been wearing it for years!'
"His trembling, withered hand produced an old copper ring from his pocket.
"My thoughts instantly filled with visions of triumph over the hated Lorquatz. To my everlasting shame, I lunged for the ring.
"Strangely enough, though I had been standing upon solid ground, the porch planks suddenly gave way beneath my feet. A warped board caught my boot, and I stumbled forward, grasping at empty air.
"'Hey!' shouted the old man. 'Don't you go trying to steal my ring! It's mine! I'm the only one who can—'
"His words were cut short by a terrible crack as the entire frame of his house, loosened by my misstep, collapsed upon him. Wood and dust exploded in every direction, and only a hastily cast spell spared me the worst of the destruction. The old man—or what remained of him—was indistinguishable from the rubble.
"The little copper ring rolled quietly out from beneath a shattered support beam until it came to rest against my boots. It had escaped the devastation entirely.
"The power of the lucky ring was mine.
"I stood for a long while, staring at its tarnished form in the dirt.
"In what I have since chosen to regard as one of the wisest moments of my life, I decided there must be other ways to defeat the Dark Lord, and turned away.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Luck: +3
- Armor Class: +3
- Saving Throws: +3
- Critical Hit Chance: +3%

Charge Ability:
- May cast Supreme Luck twice per day (+7 cumulative bonus to Luck, Saving Throws, Armor Class, and Critical Hit Chance for 1 turn; 4% chance of dying an improbable death).~
@40281 = ~Supreme Luck~

@4029 = ~Mercy and Malevolence~
@4030 = ~Mercy and Malevolence were created by the Hierophant Druid Tears of Nature on the day of his ascension to the rank of Archdruid. Neither hero nor villain so much as a force of nature, Tears of Nature was as likely to end a famine by leveling a village as by magically enriching its fields. He believed that good and evil were false constructs of civilized society. In the natural world, men, fish, trees, and beasts were all of equal worth.
Mercy and Malevolence embody those beliefs. When wielded by one of true neutrality, healing and killing become equally effortless, showing no preference for good or evil. Whatever is granted to one must also be granted to the other.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Ability:
- Wisdom: +3

Charge Abilities:
- Mercy may cast Restorative Battle Fury, Mass Regeneration (2 turns), or Mass Resurrection three times per day.
- Malevolence may cast Mass Draining Degeneration (drains 4 levels, no Saving Throw), Horrid Wilting, or Mass Finger of Death three times per day.

Special:
- Spells cast by Mercy affect allies only.
- Spells cast by Malevolence affect enemies only.
- All effects are instantaneous.

Usable By:
- True Neutral characters~
@40301 = ~Mercy~
@40302 = ~Malevolence~
@40303 = ~Mass Regeneration~
@40304 = ~Restorative Battle Fury~
@40305 = ~Mass Draining Degeneration~
@40306 = ~Mass Finger of Death~

@4031 = ~Vinzzeti's Masterpiece~
@4032 = ~Master alchemist Donado Vinzzeti crafted this shimmering suit of scale mail not as an instrument of war, but as an expression of his art. Never having studied the arcane craft of enchantment, Vinzzeti sought to create the perfect elemental defense through alchemy alone. He labored over every scale, every seam, and every rivet, infusing each component with a distilled essence of elemental protection. The project consumed nearly every waking moment of his life for almost a year.
The result, though impressive, never satisfied the alchemist. His masterpiece was functional, but far from perfect. So disheartened was he by its shortcomings that he ultimately cast the armor aside, declaring it the crowning achievement of his failure to master his craft.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Elemental Resistance: +60%

Armor Class: 1
Weight: 15
Required Strength: 8

Not Usable By:
 Mage~

@4033 = ~Gorgamoth's Shell~
@4034 = ~A grief-stricken enchanter fashioned this unusual shield from the shell of his beloved pet, Gorgamoth. For more than two centuries, the tortoise had been both the enchanter's closest companion and his most dependable test subject. Gorgamoth was the perfect familiar for a wizard. He never complained and seemed to regard the world—and everyone in it—with the same disdain as his master.
The tortoise also possessed an uncanny resistance to magic, shrugging off even the enchanter's most powerful spells. More than once, this remarkable trait saved Gorgamoth's life during one of his master's ill-tempered outbursts or less inspired experiments.
When the cantankerous old creature finally succumbed to the weight of the years, the enchanter found himself alone. Never one to indulge in self-pity, he instead resolved to put Gorgamoth's remains to good use.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Ability:
- Magic Resistance: +70%

Special:
- Enchanted to be lightweight, allowing mages to use it without penalty.

Armor Class Bonus: +3
Weight: 3
Required Strength: 8

Not Usable By:
 Druid
 Thief
 Monk

Toutes les caractéristiques : +2 (permanent)~
@4035 = ~Antediluvian Potion~
@4036 = ~This potion was recovered from the depths of the Seeker's Chest. At first glance, such a humble vial seemed insignificant compared to the treasures the chest's magic was said to contain, yet its effects proved to be astonishingly potent. Who could have brewed such a remarkable elixir? And who could have imagined that such a treasure had lain hidden somewhere in the world only moments before?

STATISTICS:

- All Attributes: +2 (Permanent)~

@4037 = ~Gesen's Iron Bow~
@4038 = ~The Iron Bow is one of the two legendary weapons of Gesen Khan, the mythical chieftain of the nomads of the steppes (the other being Gesen's Hammer). It is said that this great khan was the first to unite all the tribes and bring an end to their endless wars.
Among Gesen's greatest treasures was this Iron Bow, which some claim was the very first bow ever fashioned. It is said that no ordinary man possessed the strength to draw it, and none could withstand the terrible arrows it loosed. A single shaft could split an oak in two or shatter a boulder into a thousand fragments. And because it was the first of all bows, it was also the wisest and most renowned. Thus, any who so much as touched it would thereafter become a master archer.
Gesen eventually died and was buried in the heart of the steppes. Legend claims that the bow was laid to rest beside him, yet throughout the centuries many have sworn to have seen—or even wielded—it.

STATISTICS:

The bow creates its own +5 arrows and grants 4 attacks per round.

Charge Abilities:
- Lightning Bolt (10th level) twice per day
- Flame Arrow (20th level) twice per day
- Slayer Arrow (target dies if it fails a Save vs. Death at -4) once per day

THAC0: +5 (+5 from arrows)
Damage: +1 (+5 from arrows)
Weight: 7
Speed Factor: 2
Proficiency Type: Longbow
Type: Two-Handed
Required Strength: 19

Not Usable By:
 Druid
 Cleric
 Mage
 Thief
 Bard~

@4039 = ~Wand of High Arcana~
@4040 = ~Created by the Archmage Ioulaum, the Wand of High Arcana is one of the most celebrated magical relics of Netherese legend. Long ago, Ioulaum was said to have accomplished countless wonders through its masterful use. Since then, the wand has passed through the hands of many adventurers, though not all have wielded it with such noble purpose.

STATISTICS:

Charge Abilities:
- Three times per day: casts Breach, Spellstrike, and Lower Resistance (30%) on a single target
- Twice per day: grants Spell Sequencer (1 turn) and Alacrity (casting time reduced by 3) to the wielder
- Once per day: magically grants the wielder the benefits of a full Rest

Usable By:
 Any spellcaster~

@4041 = ~Ring of Immortality~
@4042 = ~Two thousand years ago, a sorcerer named Nethron sought to slay his own father, a silver dragon. Nethron had inherited great magical power through his father's blood, but not his longevity, and became obsessed with creating an artifact that would grant him eternal life. He forged a ring capable of bestowing that blessing, but it required one final ingredient to awaken its power: his father's blood. To obtain it, he gathered a band of adventurers and manipulated them into slaying the dragon.
His companions soon realized they had been deceived into murdering a noble dragon and came to understand that Nethron's own blood had corrupted him beyond all hope of redemption. Nethron slew them all save one warrior named Waxler. Waxler spent years hunting the sorcerer, and a decade later the two finally met again. The warrior took Nethron's head, but came to realize that the immortality promised by the ring could corrupt anyone. He hid the ring away, yet only a few years later found himself returning to reclaim it.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Immunity to Poison and Disease
- Immunity to Death Magic (except Disintegration)
- Hit Points Doubled
- Regenerates 3 Hit Points per round

Charge Ability:
- May cast Regeneration (20th level) upon the wearer twice per day.~

@4043 = ~Iron Ring~
@4044 = ~Three brothers and two sisters from a mining village in Cormyr were called to the front at Tilverton. Though eager to answer their king's summons, they were reluctant to leave behind their aging parents. Before departing, they had five rings forged from iron mined in their village, each enchanted to protect its wearer in battle. They wagered that, with these rings, at least one of them would survive to care for their mother and father.
Four of the rings were recovered from the ruins of the battlefield after the fortress fell.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Physical Damage Resistance: +40%
- Armor Class: +3

Charge Abilities:
- Iron Skins (8 skins) once per day
- Steel Resilience twice per day~

@4045 = ~Algonte's Antinomic Ring of Arcana~
@4046 = ~Algonte's Antinomic Ring of Arcana was created centuries ago by the adventuring wizard Algonte, who required powerful magical defenses to survive the perils of his chosen profession. With the aid of this ring, Algonte lived for several centuries, and ever since his disappearance the artifact has remained the object of an unending search.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Grants one additional 7th-level spell slot, two additional 8th-level spell slots, and three additional 9th-level spell slots.

Usable By:
 Mage~
@4047 = ~Silifaine's Blessed Ring~
@4048 = ~This ring saved the life of the wizard Silifaine more times than anyone could count. Those who crossed paths with the legendary mage told of seeing her struck by blows that should have been fatal, only for her to rise again without so much as a scratch.

After a long life spent eluding the judgment of Kelemvor, she gradually felt an ever-growing burden upon her soul. She knew her appointed hour was drawing near. During one of her journeys, she came upon a pale knight standing beside the road with one hand outstretched, palm open. With the faintest of smiles, Silifaine removed her ring and placed it into the pale knight's waiting hand. She then continued down the road, and no one ever saw her again.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Saving Throws: +5
- Casts Cure Light Wounds on the wearer whenever struck by a successful attack.~

@4049 = ~Arn's Invulnerable Mail~
@4050 = ~Few now remember the humble nation that was once enslaved by the wizard Virtos. Every champion the people sent against him had fallen before the mage. Seeing that no one else remained willing to defend their cause, the nation's priests and finest craftsmen joined their efforts to forge a suit of chain mail that would grant its wearer the courage and strength needed to defeat Virtos. When the armor was finally completed, the priests prayed that a true champion would be sent to them.
One week later, Arn, a humble priest from the south, arrived in the city. His order devoted itself to aiding the poor and the needy, and he was counted among its most virtuous servants. Upon learning of the people's suffering, he agreed to don the armor and lead their armies into battle. Virtos was overthrown with scarcely a struggle, and when the fighting was over, Arn vanished, taking the enchanted mail with him.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Physical Damage Resistance: +50%
- Elemental Resistances: +75%
- Saving Throws: +5
- Immunity to Backstab

Armor Class: 0
Weight: 10
Required Strength: 8

Not Usable By:
 Evil characters
 Druid
 Mage
 Thief~

@4051 = ~Bayle's Headband~
@4052 = ~This blessed headband once belonged to Bayle Dugallon, a Knight of Torm. When he was ordered to destroy a trio of priests of the Dark Lord, he was granted leave to choose a single item from the temple's secret vault to aid him in his mission. Already content with his sword and armor, Bayle chose this simple headband, preferring an unassuming ornament that left his face uncovered.
The mission proved far more difficult than expected and led the knight in pursuit of the Cyricists into the Lower Plane of Pandemonium. There the priests had sought refuge, for within that realm of madness they found allies worthy of the Black Sun's power.
Sustained by his unwavering faith, Bayle chanted prayers to Torm without ceasing as he crossed the infernal plane in pursuit of the dark priests. His resolve never faltered, and his quest was crowned with success. When he struck down the last of the three, he knew that his god had stood beside him, and that the headband he had been given possessed the power of a minor artifact.
Sadly, he was never able to use it again. For reasons still unknown, the knight never returned to the Prime Material Plane.
Yet the Seeker's Chest has somehow recovered the headband for you. How it came to be upon the Prime Material Plane remains a mystery.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Magic Resistance: +25%
- Armor Class: +3
- Saving Throws: +3
- Wisdom: +3
- +2 THAC0 and Damage vs. Evil creatures
- Protection from Evil
- Grants one additional divine spell slot of each level from 1st through 7th

Usable By:
 Good-aligned Paladins and Clerics~

@4053 = ~Dragonbone Armor~
@4054 = ~This armor is, by far, one of the most powerful artifacts a collector or hero could ever hope to possess. Fashioned from the enchanted bones of a dragon by Zurin Arctus, the first Imaskari battle mage, at the dawn of the First Age, it has been coveted by countless would-be owners ever since. Its magical properties grant the wearer complete immunity to fire and the power to unleash devastating flames upon their enemies.
Little is known of the King of the Underdark's role in the forging of this cuirass, though an archmage of old claimed that the king crafted it to repay a debt owed to a wandering warrior. Like its first owner, the armor never remains in one place for long.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Fire Resistance: +100%
- Fireshield

Charge Abilities:
- Fireball twice per day
- Sunfire twice per day
- Incendiary Cloud once per day

Armor Class: -3
Weight: 20
Required Strength: 15

Not Usable By:
 Bard
 Druid
 Mage
 Thief~

@4055 = ~Rajhin's Amulet~
@4056 = ~This amulet was already centuries old when it came into the possession of Rajhin, the tabaxi thief who made it famous. Rajhin was the first to realize that the artifact's powers could be used to create duplicates of himself, allowing him to perform noble deeds in one place while, elsewhere, his other selves carried out countless robberies. Thanks to the amulet, he became the greatest burglar in the history of Maztica.
How Rajhin ultimately met his end is unknown, but legend claims that the amulet eventually rebelled against the near-constant abuse of its powers. At the worst possible moment, it simply vanished, leaving Rajhin alone to face his enemies.

Charge Ability:
- Once per day, creates 3 clones (100% identical to the user) that last for 4 hours.
  (Note: Due to a game engine bug, the clones cannot be used until after saving and reloading the game, making them useless if created during combat.)~
@40561 = ~Tri-Clones~

@4057 = ~Plunifene's Destroyer~
@4058 = ~For generations, the Black Brotherhood has coveted this black-bladed sword. This legendary artifact is capable of instantly slaying—or at the very least grievously wounding—nearly any creature. History records none of its former owners, but the Black Brotherhood was recently devastated by a series of brutal internal power struggles. Many believe that Plunifene's Destroyer was somehow responsible.

STATISTICS:

Combat Ability:
- 25% chance on hit to disintegrate the target (Save vs. Spell at -2 negates; on a successful save, the target still suffers 5D6 points of Magic Damage)

THAC0: +6
Damage: 2D4 +6, +4 Magic Damage
Damage Type: Slashing
Weight: 4
Speed Factor: 2
Proficiency Type: Bastard Sword
Type: One-Handed
Required Strength: 11

Not Usable By:
 Druid
 Cleric
 Mage
 Thief~

@4059 = ~Cloak of Multiplication~
@4060 = ~The most powerful wizards command a vast arsenal of devastating spells with which to confront the greatest of threats, yet their greatest weakness has always been their own physical frailty. Unable to wear armor without severely hindering their spellcasting, they must seek other means of protection. Some devote themselves to rigorous physical training, but most prefer to enchant their robes or rely upon magical rings rather than burden themselves with armor. Even so, few can long withstand the assault of a seasoned warrior or the arrows of a master archer.
The priests of Mystra, well aware of this dilemma, devised these cloaks, blessed by the goddess herself. They surround the wearer with a constant veil of illusory duplicates, allowing most attacks to strike only phantoms. Needless to say, a wizard fortunate enough to possess such a cloak becomes even more formidable—some would say nearly invincible.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Ability:
- 4 permanent Mirror Images~

@4061 = ~Viccilo's Hatchet~
@4062 = ~Captain Viccilo was a pirate, beloved by his crew for his sense of humor and renowned for his practical jokes. One day, the crew decided to return the favor. They staged a mutiny and marooned him on a deserted island for two days. They left him with nothing but a small hatchet for cracking coconuts, knowing full well that the island held no coconut palms.
When they finally dragged him back aboard, he was half dead from thirst. His first mate offered him a mug of water. Viccilo swallowed it in a single gulp, only to discover it was seawater. He spent the next several minutes vomiting across the deck, too weak to rise. The crew roared with laughter, and Viccilo assured them he bore no grudge.
But after brushing so close to death, Viccilo was no longer the same man. He laughed with his crew, yet beneath the surface he seethed with fury. It took him weeks to recover his strength, and when he finally did, he waited until one night when his crew slept soundly. Armed with nothing but this hatchet, he butchered every last one of them.
The hatchet is a dark gray color, its haft and blade streaked with veins of crimson coral. It is said that a fragment of Captain Viccilo's vengeful spirit lingers within the blade, and that some of its victims died not from their wounds, but from unbearable dehydration.

STATISTICS:

Combat Abilities:
- 10% chance on hit to instantly slay the target (Save vs. Death negates)
- 5% chance on hit to cast Abi-Dalzim's Horrid Wilting

THAC0: +5
Damage: 1D6 +5, +2D8 Magical Dehydration Damage
Damage Type: Slashing
Weight: 3
Speed Factor: 2
Proficiency Type: Axe
Type: One-Handed

Not Usable By:
 Druid
 Cleric
 Mage
 Thief~

@4063 = ~Gwyn's Family Ring~
@4064 = ~Gwyn was a young woman from Snowflake Vale who was betrothed to a farmer from her village. Her parents looked forward to the marriage with great joy, yet as the wedding day drew ever closer, Gwyn was plagued by dreadful nightmares in which she was suffocating. She would awaken gasping for air, her mouth hanging open, unable to move.

She spoke to her parents of the nightmares and of the crushing weight that seemed to rest upon her shoulders, growing heavier with each passing day. They assured her that her nerves would settle in time, but they never did.

On the morning of her wedding day, while the sky was still gray and a thick mist hung over the fields, Gwyn slipped away. She hoped that a long walk would calm her mind, but the farther she traveled from Crossing Valley, the lighter she felt. When she reached the Pearlwood Cliffs, she realized she had no intention of ever returning.

She spent the night at a small roadside inn, where the kindly innkeeper gave her a room free of charge. After a hot meal and a hearty mug of ale, she enjoyed the best night's sleep she had known in years. The next morning, her hostess directed her toward Caer Westphal.

She reached the city and quickly found the harbor. She possessed nothing of value save her engagement ring, which she traded for passage across the sea to the mainland.

What Gwyn believed to be nothing more than a family heirloom was later revealed to be a powerful sarrukh relic, one that reveals its full potential only when worn by an Entropist.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- +100 to all rolls on the Entropic Hiatus table
- Doubles the number of memorized 1st-level spells

Usable Only By:
 Mage~

@4065 = ~Tuerny the Merciless' Iron Urn~
@4066 = ~Throughout all of human history, Tuerny the Merciless alone came to embody Evil itself. A powerful wizard, he served as advisor to the king of a small realm. Believing the monarch to be weak and foolish, Tuerny had him murdered in his sleep, along with the entire royal family, before seizing the throne for himself. With his immense magical power, he either charmed the kingdom's soldiers or enslaved them to his will. He then summoned vile tanar'ri and unleashed them upon his helpless subjects, only to discover that he could not control them. Determined to bend the fiends to his command, he devoted himself to crafting an artifact capable of imposing his will upon them. When at last he deemed it complete, he summoned a mighty tanar'ri, subdued it, and imprisoned it within his Iron Urn.

By then, however, the neighboring kingdoms had learned of the new tyrant's atrocities. They raised great armies to crush Tuerny and his infernal servants. The war that followed was unimaginably bloody, dragging on for years without either side gaining the upper hand. In desperation, Tuerny finally opened his Urn—but the imprisoned tanar'ri immediately turned upon him. A terrible scream echoed across the battlefield as Tuerny was transformed into a hideous monster before vanishing, together with the demon and the artifact.

Charge Abilities:
- Summon Marilith once per day for 4 hours
- Power Word: Stun once per day
- Chain Lightning once per day.~

@40661 = ~Iron Urn~
@40662 = ~This urn appears to be a prison. It seems to hold a creature of tremendous power captive.

Weight: 1~

@4067 = ~Edhan's Combat Belt~
@4068 = ~A barbarian named Edhan asked his tribe's shaman to enchant this belt, for he risked his life every day hunting young white dragons across the tundra of Icewind Dale. The perilous occupation earned him more gold than most barbarians could ever dream of, but as the years passed, Edhan's reflexes began to fail him. In his later years, the belt proved an invaluable aid.

Even so, shortly after celebrating his fifty-sixth winter, Edhan fell to the combined assault of two white dragons. The warriors of the Great Dracosire tribe managed to gather what remained of his body, but rather than bury him, they chose to sell his belt along with the rest of his equipment...

STATISTICS:

Equipped Ability:
- All attacks made by the wearer are automatic critical hits and inflict maximum damage~

@4069 = ~Maorec Ertui~
@4070 = ~Passed down through a tribe of the Great Glacier, this quiver was said to have been blessed by the gods, for no huntress who carried it ever returned empty-handed. It was also said that its supply of arrows could never be exhausted.

Rather than entrust the quiver to a single person, the tribe believed that every member should share in such a divine gift. But one young huntress coveted both the quiver and its power, believing she could seize control of the tribe if the artifact belonged to her alone. One day, while Maorec Ertui had been entrusted to her for the hunt, she slipped away from her companions and fled with it.

STATISTICS:

- Unlimited +4 Stunning Fire Arrows (THAC0 +4, Damage: 1D6 +4 Piercing +1D6 Fire +1D4 Crushing; Save vs. Spell or Stunned for 2 rounds)~

@4071 = ~Nature's Wrath~
@4072 = ~A circle of militant druids devoted to Silvanus created this staff to unleash nature's vengeance upon all who despoil the forests and show contempt for the druidic order. The druids of Silvanus most often wielded it against the priests of Malar and other gods of savage fury.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Doubles the number of priest spells the wielder can memorize
- Spell Sequencer

Combat Abilities:
- Casts Insect Plague on hit
- 5% chance to cast Creeping Doom instead
- 2% chance to cast Creeping Doom instead

THAC0: +6
Damage: 1D6 +6
Damage Type: Crushing
Weight: 4
Speed Factor: 0
Proficiency Type: Quarterstaff
Type: Two-Handed
Required Strength: 5

Usable Only By:
 Druid~

@4073 = ~Unerring Crossbow~
@4074 = ~The Unerring Crossbow was the invention of Engar Resc, a Damaran prospector who discovered a rich vein of silver in an otherwise empty cave. He had only just begun mining it when the cave's owner—according to Engar, the largest bear he had ever seen—found him there and charged.

Engar had come prepared and emptied his repeating crossbow into the charging beast, but even the finest weapon seldom strikes exactly where it is aimed. He was horribly mauled, surviving only by feigning death.

Vowing never to find himself in such a predicament again, Engar spent part of his newfound wealth forging a new crossbow that would never miss its mark and could halt the charge of even the fiercest bear. The final result surpassed all his expectations, and the bear of the silver cave was riddled with bolts before it could so much as raise a paw.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Attacks per Round: Set to 4
- Improved Haste
- Every bolt fired from this crossbow slows the target for 3 rounds (no saving throw)

Damage: +3
THAC0: +20
Weight: 5
Speed Factor: 0
Proficiency Type: Crossbow
Type: Two-Handed
Required Strength: 8

Not Usable By:
 Druid
 Cleric
 Mage~

@4075 = ~Marauder's Helm~
@4076 = ~A notorious Murghôm raider came upon a small forge operated by gnomes devoted to Flandal Steelskin. He slaughtered them all save for one smith, who had hidden herself inside a supply crate. The gnome called out to the raider and offered him a bargain. She would forge the mightiest helm he had ever seen if he swore to spare her life. The raider granted her one week and demanded that the helm honor his patron, Garagos.

The smith worked without rest until the final evening, then placed the completed helm just inside the unlocked door of the forge. At dawn the following morning, the helm had vanished—and the shrine of Flandal was never plundered again.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- +4 Melee and Missile Damage
- +4 Attacks per Round
- Armor Class: +4

Charge Ability:
- Casts Steel Dance four times per day~

@4077 = ~Kalimulin's Eternal Bolt~
@4078 = ~The Eternal Bolt was originally discovered among the possessions of a member of the Doomguard, though she was almost certainly not its first owner. After falling into the hands of the mad electromancer Kalimulin, it was re-enchanted to satisfy the wizard's obsessive desire to let everyone experience the bite of lightning.

The bolt is covered with runes accompanied by tiny elemental and binding symbols. These are believed to be the source of its ability to strike multiple foes with a single shot.

STATISTICS:

- Recreates itself in the crossbow after every shot
- On hit, releases a Chain Lightning that deals 4D6 Electrical Damage (Save vs. Spell for half)

THAC0: +4
Damage: 1D8, +1D10 Electrical Damage
Damage Type: Missile (Piercing)
Weight: 0
Launcher: Crossbow~

@4079 = ~Ring of Minor Spells~
@4080 = ~Little is known of this ring's origins, though the markings surrounding its gemstone suggest that it is several centuries old. To most, it appears to be nothing more than an ordinary old piece of jewelry. Yet in the hands of one gifted in the arcane arts, the ring shines with a brilliant light and grows warm to the touch.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Ability:
- The wearer regains all 1st-, 2nd-, and 3rd-level spells every round.

Usable Only By:
 Mage
 Bard~

@4081 = ~Dillpater's Grimoire~
@4082 = ~The infamous archmage Dillpater was a prolific author who trained many apprentices, though none ever achieved the same fame—or longevity—as their master. Those fortunate enough to study one of Dillpater's grimoires generally describe its diagrams and script as utterly indecipherable, yet none can deny the overwhelming power of the spells contained within.

STATISTICS:

Charge Abilities:
- Twice per day, the wielder may cast one of the following spells inscribed within the grimoire:

**Fatal Rage:** Target summoned creature or ally: The target becomes Enraged and Berserk, gains 10 attacks per round, but loses 2 Constitution each round.

**Mark of Fire:** Target creature (target's Magic Resistance is reduced by 30% against this spell): No saving throw. Fire Resistance -100%.

**Dillpater's Terrible Cleaver:** Target creature: Save vs. Spell or die. On a successful save, the target's current hit points are reduced by half.

**Spider's Dominion:** Target a non-summoned spider: Save vs. Spell at -4 or the spider becomes the mage's permanent companion and may follow the party between areas.

**Summon Beholder:** Summons a beholder. 15% chance to summon a second.

**Curse of Certain Death:** Area of effect: Enemies suffer -4 to all saving throws. They must then Save vs. Spell or suffer an additional -4 to all saving throws, followed by a Save vs. Death or suffer a further -4 penalty to Save vs. Death (for a total penalty of -12 if both saves fail).

**Vaal Skeleton Army:** Summons 55 skeletons of varying strength, including a skeleton general who hastens the others.

**Magical Rest:** Refreshes all of the caster's spells and special abilities.

**Medusa's Eye:** Caster gains a petrifying gaze attack usable once per round at will (Save vs. Petrification negates).

**Chain Images:** Caster gains Improved Alacrity? Wait no—creates one enhanced image, then another every 2 rounds (maximum of 5 images).

**Camouflage:** Improved Invisibility plus complete immunity to all forms of detection.

**Prismatic Orb:** Target suffers all effects of Prismatic Spray simultaneously.

**Invulnerability:** Caster becomes immortal, immune to all death effects, and automatically succeeds on all saving throws for 4 rounds.

**Deathshade Mist:** Cloud effect: Summons 1D3 Phantom Assassins each round.

**Create Clone:** Creates a 100% clone of the caster. (Requires a save/reload before the clone can be controlled due to an engine limitation.)

**Nazzer's Mantle of Nullification:** Area around the caster: Every second, all magic is dispelled at 100% effectiveness. No magic or spells function within the area.

**Illusory Company:** Area around the caster: Every party member gains a Simulacrum.

**Erasure:** Target spellcaster: Save vs. Spell at -2 or forgets all memorized spells.

**Dillpater's Transformation:** Tenser's Transformation, Strength set to 19, +1 Attack per Round, and spellcasting cannot be interrupted.

**Orb of Absolute Destruction:** Multi-elemental Fireball dealing 24D6 damage. Creatures at the center must Save vs. Death or be disintegrated.

Usable Only By:
 Mage~
@40829 = ~Dillpater's Grimoire

The infamous archmage Dillpater was a remarkably prolific author who trained numerous apprentices, though none ever matched their master's fame—or his extraordinary longevity. Those fortunate enough to study Dillpater's grimoires invariably describe the diagrams and handwriting as utterly indecipherable, yet none dispute the overwhelming power of the spells they contain.

STATISTICS:

Charge Abilities:

Twice per day, the wielder may cast one of the spells inscribed within the grimoire:

Fatal Rage: Targeted summoned creature or ally becomes enraged and berserk, gaining 10 attacks per round, but loses 2 Constitution each round.

Mark of Fire: Target creature (its Magic Resistance is reduced by 30% against this spell); no saving throw; Fire Resistance -100%.

Dillpater's Grisly Cleaver: Target creature must save vs. spell or die instantly. On a successful save, its current hit points are reduced by half.

Spider's Subjugation: Cast upon a non-summoned spider. Save vs. spell at -4 or the spider becomes the mage's permanent companion and may accompany them between areas.

Summon Beholder: Summons a beholder. 15% chance to summon a second.

Curse of Certain Death: Area of effect; enemies suffer -4 to all saving throws, then must save vs. spell or suffer an additional -4 to all saving throws, then save vs. death or suffer a further -4 penalty to save vs. death (up to -12 total).

Vaal Skeleton Army: Summons 55 skeletons of varying strength, including a skeleton general who hastens the others.

Supernatural Dweomers: Caster's spellcasting time is fixed at 1.

Magical Rest: Instantly refreshes all of the caster's spells and abilities.

Medusa's Eye: Grants a once-per-round petrifying gaze attack (save vs. petrification to resist).

Chain Images: Grants Improved Invisibility Image, then creates one additional image every 2 rounds (maximum 5 images).

Camouflage: Grants Improved Invisibility and immunity to all effects that would reveal the caster.

Prismatic Orb: Inflicts every effect of Prismatic Spray simultaneously upon the target.

Invulnerability: The caster becomes immortal, immune to all forms of death, and automatically succeeds on all saving throws for 4 rounds.

Infant's Frailty: Target must save vs. spell at -4 or lose all weapon immunities, spell immunities, and have every resistance reduced to 0%.

Gloomdeath Mist: Creates a cloud that summons 1d3 Phantom Assassins each round.

Create Clone: Creates a perfect clone of the caster. (Requires saving and reloading before the clone becomes usable.)

Nazzer's Mantle of Nullification: Surrounds the caster with an aura that continuously dispels magic at 100% strength every second, preventing all magic and spellcasting within the area.

Illusory Company: Every party member gains a Mislead duplicate.

Erasure: Target spellcaster must save vs. spell at -2 or forget all memorized spells.

Dillpater's Transformation: Grants Tenser's Transformation, Strength 19, +1 attack per round, and allows uninterrupted spellcasting.

Special: A mage may permanently learn every spell contained within the grimoire. Doing so completely drains the tome's magical energy, destroying it in the process.

Usable By:

Mage~
@40821 = ~Cast One of the Spells~
@40822 = ~Learn the Spells~

@4083 = ~Spelldrinker~
@4084 = ~Created by the legendary order of battle mages known as the Crimson Seers, this enchanted sword ensures that its wielder is never deprived of magical power. The tiny runes etched across its blade are potent arcane catalysts, absorbing magical energy from every possible source.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:

Permanent Spell Trap
Immunity to spell drain
Each round, the wielder regains one random spell.

Combat Abilities:

Striking a wizard or sorcerer causes them to forget one spell of the highest spell level they know, while allowing the wielder to recover one spell of the highest spell level they know.
20% chance on hit to recover one random spell.

Damage: 1d8 +5
THAC0: +5
Damage Type: Slashing
Weight: 4
Speed Factor: 0
Proficiency Type: Long Sword
Type: One-Handed
Requires: 6 Strength

Usable By:

Bard
Fighter/Mage
Fighter/Mage/Thief
Dual-classed Fighter › Mage~

@4085 = ~Yratnuthul's Grimoire of Flames~
@4086 = ~Though worn with age, Yratnuthul's grimoire contains a vast reservoir of spells, every page filled with instructions, warnings, and observations on the mastery of fire. The tome smells of ash, and its pages are riddled with scattered burn marks. Muddy fingerprints stain several leaves, and at least one diagram was drawn in blood long since dried. Despite this, the binding remains sturdy, and the arcane symbols and annotations throughout the grimoire are written in fresh black ink.

STATISTICS:

Studying this grimoire allows the reader to learn approximately twenty fire spells as though they were one spell level lower than normal.
Special: The grimoire may only be studied once. Afterward, it vanishes, only to reappear somewhere else in the world.

Usable By:

Mage
Bard~

@40861 = ~Summon Archfire Elemental~
@40862 = ~Summon Archfire Elemental (Conjuration)

Level: 8
Range: 10 yd.
Duration: 10 rounds
Casting Time: 1 round
Area of Effect: Special
Saving Throw: None

This spell, created by the pyromancer Abznard, directly summons Zaaman Rul, the Prince of Elemental Fire. Zaaman Rul serves the caster faithfully for 10 rounds.~

@4087 = ~Helm of the King of the Dead~
@4088 = ~Shelgoth, a powerful non-human lich who "lived" during the fall of Netheril, terrorized the refugees of the shattered empire as they wandered the region known as Moander's Footprint. His undead servants and monstrous beasts struck from the rocky heights, spreading terror wherever they appeared, and more than a hundred mages were forced to combine their powers to defeat him. After finally destroying the lich, they decided to fashion a magical helm from his skull. Some historians speculate that these mages may have sought to recreate an artifact akin to the infamous Horned Crown, though the helm's true origins and the methods used to forge it have been lost to history.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Ability:
Protection from Undead

Charge Abilities:

Cast Boneguard twice per day
Cast Walking Dead twice per day
Cast Control Undead (30-ft. radius) twice per day~

@40881 = ~Boneguard~

@4089 = ~Sarat-Tapai's Battle Robe~
@4090 = ~Sarat-Tapai of Cormyr was born with exceptional strength of both body and mind. Chosen at an early age to study the arcane arts, he dutifully mastered the discipline and became an extraordinarily powerful battle mage, though it never fulfilled him. What he truly desired was to stand shoulder to shoulder with warriors and settle his battles with a quarterstaff.

He devoted many years to creating a robe worthy of that dream.

Woven from fine iridium threads and imbued with powerful enchantments, this robe grants its wearer every benefit of Tenser's Transformation without preventing spellcasting, along with several additional enhancements. Long sought after by fighter/mages, it has been considered lost ever since Sarat-Tapai's death.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:

Armor Class: 1
Melee THAC0: +10
Melee Damage: +2
Hit Points: +40
Attacks per Round: +1
Strength set to 19

Weight: 5

Usable By:

Mage~

@4091 = ~Soul of the Stars Sling~
@4092 = ~This remarkable sling is fashioned with silver leather straps and a pouch woven from threads of an unknown greenish alloy. During the age of the Kingdom of Asram, the powerful human mage Tellialachus sought to create a worthy gift for Lord Adilonn. Using a mighty spell—now lost to history—he journeyed into the Astral Plane, where he claimed to have torn open a rift in space itself. Drawing upon the power beyond the stars, he imbued a masterfully crafted sling with celestial might.
Upon returning from his extraordinary voyage, Tellialachus found himself unwilling to part with his creation. But Adilonn was already expecting the gift and dispatched his personal champions to retrieve it. Tellialachus fled, never to be seen again.
The sling resurfaced some ten years later in the hands of a centaur, who single-handedly drove back the hordes of yetis descending from the north during the Year of Silent Cries. Since then, the Soul of the Stars Sling has appeared only sporadically, at one point even falling into the possession of a tribe of bugbears. Its whereabouts remained a mystery until the Seeker's Chest uncovered it once more.

STATISTICS:

The sling creates its own ammunition in the form of Star Fragments, which may be fired in one of two modes:

A Star Fragment deals 10d6 damage to a single target it strikes.
A Star Fragment explodes on impact, dealing 4d6 damage to all creatures within a 15-foot radius.

Star Fragment damage is half fire and half magical.

THAC0: +5
Damage: +5 (missile)
Weight: 0
Speed Factor: 0
Proficiency Type: Sling
Type: One-Handed Weapon
Requires: 3 Strength~

@4093 = ~Chalice of Dawn~
@4094 = ~In the world's earliest days, the sun blazed with such fury that even stone burned beneath its light. Dragons alone could survive upon the earth, their ancient bodies enduring the relentless flames. In time they grew weary of solitude and pleaded with the sun to temper its fire so that other forms of life might flourish.

At first the sun refused. Yet the dragons' longing for companions moved it deeply, and it agreed to spare the world from an eternity of fire. To commemorate this covenant between sky and earth—and to protect the younger races from the might of the elder dragons—the gold dragons forged the Chalices of Dawn.
Centuries later, seven great mortal rulers were entrusted with these chalices as symbols of the dragons' faith. Each vessel bestowed the sun's blessings: prosperity, health, protection, and even immortality. But the rulers' greed drew too much of the sun's power into mortal hands. Eventually, war erupted over possession of the chalices. Two were destroyed, two remain guarded by the oldest and most powerful dragons, and the remaining three have been lost, forgotten alike by dragons and by the sun itself.

STATISTICS:

Charge Abilities:

Cast Resurrection twice per day
Cast Heal four times per day
Cast Sunray ten times per day~

@4095 = ~Essence of Fire~
@4096 = ~The product of alchemical genius and extraplanar madness, the original Essence of Fire was a rare concoction first created in a city that now lies in ruins beneath the southern deserts. Legends tell of the mad alchemist Abnezgar, whose potions were said to be far purer than any known today. Unsatisfied with the wealth and immortality his craft had already earned him, he set his sights on overthrowing Caliph Sahl.
Lacking both the Caliph's armies and his cunning, Abnezgar decided that the surest path to victory was to summon the terrible efreet whom Sahl had once driven from his realm. Concealing himself with his own alchemical brews, Abnezgar journeyed to the City of Brass, where he slew countless elementals and salamanders, distilling their very essence. The efreet discovered his presence only after he slew one of their own, the Pasha Elamash, and escaped back to the Material Plane with his prize.
The enraged efreet reduced the Caliph's glorious city to glass and sand before hunting down and killing Abnezgar. Yet they never found Elamash nor the alchemist's hidden laboratory. Stranger still, Abnezgar's body refused to burn upon his funeral pyre...
This potion follows the legendary principles behind Abnezgar's original Essence of Fire. Without his unmatched genius to perfect it, however, it remains only an imperfect imitation.

STATISTICS:

Fire Resistance: +75%
Grants numerous fire-based abilities, each usable 1 to 3 times per day~

@4097 = ~Silent Death~
@4098 = ~Juliena do Ancenze dearly loved her lover Bruno, a man as clever as he was deceitful. As Bruno's debts grew far beyond his ability to repay them, his creditors decided that his death would serve as a useful warning to other would-be debtors.
Bruno was murdered in his own bed. His killers deliberately left behind the weapon used in the crime—this very dagger—as a grim message. Armed with nothing but the bloodstained blade and a broken heart, Juliena hunted down each of the murderers and avenged her lover. Her later career as a highly successful bounty hunter would become the stuff of legend.

STATISTICS:

Combat Abilities:

Silences the target for 1 turn (Save vs. Spell at -4 negates)
Stuns the target for 1 turn (Save vs. Spell negates)
Poisons the target, dealing 1 point of damage per second for 1 turn (Save vs. Poison negates)
Special: The dagger automatically returns to its thrower.

THAC0: +4
Damage: 1d6 +4
Damage Type: Missile (Piercing)
Weight: 1
Speed Factor: 0
Proficiency Type: Dagger
Type: One-Handed Weapon~


@4099 = ~Magivore~
@40100 = ~This tiny fey serpent is a symbiotic creature that coils itself around the finger of any spellcaster willing to accept it. It feeds upon the residual magical energies left behind by spellcasting and thrives by consuming the arcane residue generated by its host. In return, it uses its psychic powers to project a protective field of energy around its companion.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:

Spell Sequencer
Armor Class: +4
Magic Resistance: +25%

Usable By:

Mage
Bard
Priest
Druid~

@40101 = ~Thol'gor's Arm~
@40102 = ~Thol'gor's Arm is the healthy right arm of an ogre, covered in strange blue and green tattoos. At the shoulder joint is a thick mass of short, writhing tentacles. This living creation is a graft.

If the tentacles are pressed against an empty shoulder socket, they immediately attach themselves to the new host. If placed against an intact shoulder, they burrow into the muscle and begin to take hold. For the next turn, the host possesses two right arms, though neither is functional. During this time, the host's original arm withers away until it finally falls off. Once fully attached, Thol'gor's Arm cannot be removed without killing its host.

The arm is intelligent, possessing an Ego of 15. It can communicate with its bearer only through dreams. While the host is awake, nothing unusual occurs. But once asleep, the bearer dreams of vast green landscapes, dense forests, and breathtaking woodland glades. The details always fade upon waking, though the feeling of wonder remains. Occasionally, the arm speaks during these dreams, always appearing as a floating ogress clad in robes woven from leaves, her words delivered in a clear, crystalline voice.

STATISTICS:

Special: The arm must be grafted onto the wearer. This is a permanent, one-time use. During the first turn after grafting, the bearer is unable to attack or cast spells.

Equipped Abilities (after one turn):

Strength is set to 20
Grants eight magical tattoos, each allowing the bearer to cast its associated druid spell once per day:
Neutralize Poison
Call Woodland Beings
Iron Skins
Insect Plague
Regeneration
Animal Summoning
Nature's Beauty
Storm of Vengeance~

@401021 = ~Severed Arm~
@401022 = ~This arm is strangely full of life, as though it had only just been severed. It was cleanly cut off at the shoulder—most likely by a scythe—and small tentacles writhe constantly from the wound. Intricate magical tattoos cover the entire limb, stretching from the wrist to what remains of the shoulder.~

@40103 = ~Mirella's Heart Keys~

@40104 = ~This trio of magical keys fit together like a small puzzle and form the heart of the golem known as Mirella. Mirella was the creation of Giludob, a somewhat eccentric but brilliantly gifted gnome artificer. In his workshop, he crafted countless unusual yet marvelous magical tools and devices. His greatest masterpiece was Mirella, his personal guardian golem. She followed him everywhere, ensured his safety, and assisted him with many of his tasks.
This arrangement might have lasted forever had a failed experiment not caused an explosion that killed Giludob. Left without purpose, Mirella entered a dormant state. Imperishable and ageless, she remained there, frozen in place for eternity, until a thief named Tillput—slightly more skilled than most—discovered Giludob's hidden workshop and inadvertently awakened her. Unable to damage the golem, Tillput fled. Obsessed with the wonders he had glimpsed inside the workshop, he later assembled an expedition of fellow thieves, and together they finally managed to destroy Mirella.
When the golem shattered, Tillput discovered among the gemstone fragments a tiny heart-shaped key. Unwilling to risk facing the golem again, he sold the key to a passing merchant and soon forgot about it. The merchant was killed some time later in an ambush by black treants, and the key vanished along with the rest of his treasures. If only either of them had known that merely assembling the three keys while speaking the golem's name aloud was enough to summon Mirella and bind her to the service of the keys' owner...

Abilities:

Summons Mirella, the Pink Diamond Golem. Mirella can accompany the wielder of the keys across different areas. If she is destroyed, the keys can be recovered from her remains and used again after recharging overnight.

Mirella's Statistics:

Hit Dice: 14 (60 HP)
Armor Class: 0
Strength: 22
THAC0: 7 + Strength bonus
Attack: One attack dealing 4d10 bludgeoning damage.
Resistances: Immune to normal weapons and magical weapons of +2 enchantment or lower; immune to fire, acid, and electricity; golem immunities; 20% resistance to slashing and piercing damage.
Vulnerability: 20% vulnerability to bludgeoning damage.
Special Abilities: May cast Prismatic Spray three times and Sunray once.~
@401041 = ~Mirella~
@401042 = ~Ring of Keys~

@401043 = ~The Chest has led you to a ring of keys. This is supposed to be a powerful magical artifact? There's no way to know without identifying it.~

@40105 = ~Cannibal Gloves~

@40106 = ~These gloves appear to be fashioned from shriveled flesh. At random intervals, they tighten ever so slightly around their wearer's fingers, as though some diseased heart still pumped within them. The origin of the flesh remains unknown, and judging by its marbled coloration, it is impossible to tell whether it once belonged to a single creature or several.

Whenever the gloves come into contact with a creature, they bite into it, restoring vitality to their wearer.

Equipped Abilities:
- Unarmed attacks deal an additional 2d3 piercing damage and heal the wearer for the same amount of hit points.~

@40107 = ~Ring of Devastation~

@40108 = ~The Ring of Devastation is one of the most mysterious magical artifacts ever discovered. Almost nothing is known of its origins, as no surviving text mentions it, though many believe it dates back to Netheril's golden age. The only known legend recounts its use by a mighty wizard during the assault on Castle Dragonspear. Its bearer wielded the ring to defeat devils and help retake the fortress. Since then, the ring has appeared on Toril only a handful of times.

Equipped Abilities:
- Damage dealt: +40%

Charge Ability:
- Once per day, increases all damage dealt by +100% for 4 rounds.~

@40109 = ~Ring of Rebuttal~

@40110 = ~This ring, which required years of painstaking craftsmanship and an enormous payment to commission, was created for the half-elf Bassel-Bar Dov. A renowned fighter/mage/cleric, celebrated countless times for his glorious victories, he took great offense whenever specialists of lesser renown were promoted over him.

Once the ring was completed, he believed he could settle such disputes through magical or martial duels. He realized his mistake during the very first magical confrontation, when three Comets crashed down upon him before he had even finished casting his opening spell. Powerful though he was, he lacked one crucial defense: immunity to Time Stop.

Equipped Abilities:
- Doubles the number of arcane spells the wearer can memorize.
- Doubles the number of divine spells the wearer can memorize.
- +5 caster level and +5 to saving throws.
- +50% THAC0 and hit points.

Usable only by:
 Fighter/Mage/Cleric~

@40111 = ~Ring of the Sixth Ring~

@40112 = ~This ring originates from another plane. It was forged on the Concordant Opposition by a resourceful wizard who discovered how to exploit the plane's unique properties. It was later stolen by a thief who believed he had made the perfect score—until the Red Death caught up with him. Since then, the ring should have lain within one of the Chambers of Evidence, but through some strange twist of fate it found its way to the Prime Material Plane. And now, it belongs to you.

Equipped Abilities:
- Immunity to spells of levels 7 through 9 (including immunity to Time Stop).~

@40113 = ~Naga Crown~
@40114 = ~There are several artifacts of this kind. All were crafted in ages long past by the yuan-ti.

Equipped Abilities:
- Doubles the number of arcane spells the wearer can memorize.
- Permanent Spell Turning.
- Permanent Detect Invisibility.

Charge Abilities:
- Ten times per day: Teleport Field.
- Once per day: Reptile Domination (save vs. spell at –6 or be dominated for 3 turns).~

@401141 = ~Reptile Domination~

@40115 = ~The Saint's Mace~
@40116 = ~This object, which appears to be nothing more than an ordinary rusty mace, is in fact a powerful artifact when wielded by one who is righteous.

STATISTICS:

Charge Ability:
- Divine Ray, usable at will once per round.

Combat Abilities:
- Any undead struck must succeed on a save vs. death at –4 or be instantly destroyed.

THAC0: +5
Damage: 1D6 +7, +2D6 vs. evil creatures, +2D6 vs. chaotic creatures
Damage Type: Crushing
Weight: 4
Speed Factor: 2
Proficiency Type: Mace
Type: One-handed
Requires: 6 Strength

Not Usable By:
 Evil characters
 Chaotic characters
 Druid
 Mage
 Thief~

@40117 = ~Starstone~

@40118 = ~This large stone came from the depths of space and almost certainly crashed onto Toril. It is speckled with tiny brown particles that glow with a reddish light whenever an arcanist holds it. A sufficiently gifted wizard can harness the stone's resonance with arcane power to accomplish extraordinary feats.

STATISTICS:

Charge Ability:
- Four times per day, the wielder may shape the stuff of the starry void through the stone to cast any spell they know.

Required Intelligence: 18

Weight: 4

Usable only by:
 Mage~

@40119 = ~The Endless Book~
@40120 = ~The Endless Book was created by a largely forgotten wizard, so obscure that only the name Magus has survived the passage of centuries. No firsthand account reveals who he was, what ambitions drove him, or even how he crafted the Book. However, the few sages who have studied the artifact agree that, judging by its properties, Magus sought to create an object of immense power. There is every reason to believe his ultimate goal was world domination—or something equally grandiose.
Whatever Magus's ambitions may have been, however, his skill as a wizard proved unequal to the task. He created a powerful artifact, but one that fell far short of perfection. Some legends claim his hunger for power made him rush his work, choosing haste where patience was required. Unaware of his mistakes, he forged an artifact that devastated the entire region the first time he attempted to use it. Instead of granting him the power he desired, the Endless Book completed his descent into madness.
Since Magus's time, the Book has resurfaced on several occasions. Sometimes its appearance has brought salvation; at other times, utter catastrophe. It is said to have granted the wizard Vorst Dircson the power needed to overthrow the Lich King of the North. It is also blamed for the epidemic of insanity that struck the mages of Halverston's University of Fire—a wave of madness that ended with the academy exploding and the city's harbor being destroyed. Similar incidents suggest that the Book is an artifact meant for wizards, though even they employ it only at great peril.
No one knows where the artifact currently lies. Charlatans frequently sell counterfeit copies to overly trusting spellcasters, a deception made easier by the well-known fact that magical methods of detection and verification are ineffective against true artifacts.

STATISTICS:

- Five times per day, the Book allows its wielder to cast one of the spells inscribed upon its pages.
- Whenever the Book is opened, the reader is automatically affected by every spell marked with an asterisk (*).
- Curse: Each time the Book is opened, there is a 5% chance that everyone in the area is struck by Abi-Dalzim's Horrid Wilting.

Spells inscribed within the Book:
Conjure Fire Elemental
Finger of Death
Spell Turning
True Sight
Power Word, Stun
Symbol, Fear
Call Woodland Beings*
Power Word, Blind
Emotion: Hopelessness
Flame Arrow
Regeneration*
Lower Resistance
Dispel Magic
Improved Haste*
Tenser's Transformation
Feeblemind
Confusion
Death Fog
Stoneskin*
Aerial Servant
Harm
Earthquake
Strength of One / Giant Strength*
Insect Plague
Mirror Image (8 images, target)*
Spirit Armor*
Protection from Magical Energy*

Usable only by:
 Mage~
@401201 = ~Giant Strength~
@40121 = ~Universal Magic Amulet: Oglorane~
@40122 = ~A Universal Magic Amulet can reproduce the effects of any arcane spell of the spell level to which it is attuned. All Universal Amulets were created by Driventill of Dothion. Each is unique, bears its own name, and is linked to a specific tier of magic.
This one is called **Oglorane** and is capable of reproducing epic spells.

Charge Ability:
- Three times per day, may cast any existing epic spell.~

@40123 = ~Eviscerator~
@40124 = ~What's this doing here?

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- Armor Class: -20
- All ability scores set to 25

Special:
- Counts as a +5 weapon.
- Usable by everyone.

THAC0: +20
Damage: 4D20 +20
Damage Type: Slashing
Weight: 0
Speed Factor: 1
Proficiency Type: Axe
Type: One-handed
Required Strength: 0~

@40125 = ~Hans's Annihilator~
@40126 = ~No mercenary ever embodied the philosophy of the Carnage Hand more completely than Hans Elechbert. He was both an unwavering instrument of violence and the finest cavalry officer in the company. His commanders employed Hans and his mounted troops much as one would employ a cannon. Mounted on powerful warhorses and clad in heavy armor, they shattered enemy lines with a single, devastating charge.
Hans's helmet, designed so that his men could recognize him through dust and blood alike, became a symbol of certain death for any warrior brave—or foolish—enough to stand against the mercenaries of the Hand. When Hans finally fell in battle, the few surviving enemies beheaded him. Legend claims that his severed head continued to shout and bellow from within the helmet, commanding his riders to charge and grant no quarter. The head was buried without ceremony, while Hans's body was left for the crows. The helmet, however, had not yet reached the end of its journey.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Ability:
- All of the wearer's melee attacks have a 30% chance to annihilate the target if it fails a save vs. death at +4.~

@40127 = ~Daer'Ragh's Aura Cleansing~
@40128 = ~Daer'Ragh's Aura Cleansing (All Schools)

Level: 4
Range: 0
Duration: 1 round per 2 levels
Casting Time: 4
Area of Effect: Caster
Saving Throw: None

The use of magic causes magical residue to accumulate around the spellcaster, preventing even the most powerful wizards from casting spells in rapid succession.
Daer'Ragh's Aura Cleansing disperses this residue at the beginning of the caster's next spell, allowing spells to be cast in much quicker succession.~

@40129 = ~Daer'Ragh's Mental Restoration~
@40130 = ~Daer'Ragh's Mental Restoration (All Schools)

Level: 4
Range: 0
Duration: Instantaneous
Casting Time: 4
Area of Effect: Caster
Saving Throw: None

Wizards normally require a quiet place to rest and recover the spells they have previously cast. Such a place is not always available.

Daer'Ragh's Mental Restoration allows the caster to simulate a full night's rest, instantly restoring all memorized spells and refreshing all special abilities without the need to actually rest.~

@40131 = ~Gauntlets of Assassination~
@40132 = ~Long ago, an assassin from Iriaebor became so accomplished at her trade that she lost all interest in money. Years of lucrative contracts had earned her more wealth than she could ever spend. As age caught up with her, however, she began to fear the gradual decline of her abilities.
She trained relentlessly, day after day, yet each passing year her movements seemed a little slower and her hand a little less steady. While she struggled desperately to preserve her fading skills, her rivals—and eventually even her own apprentices—began to surpass her.
When she was finally persuaded to accept another contract, she demanded payment in advance. She asked not for gold, but for an item that would allow her to continue practicing her deadly craft at the height of her abilities.
These gauntlets were her payment. They served her so well that she never dared remove them for the remainder of her long career.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
- THAC0: +5
- Backstab Multiplier: +2
- Stealth: +50%

Charge Ability:
- Assassination, three times per day.

Usable only by:
 Thief~

@40133 = ~Arcane Revelation Quill~
@40134 = ~This quill is said to have been fashioned by Sénikel, an archon of the Upper Planes, from one of the feathers of his own wings. Sénikel was responsible for providing magical equipment to the celestial warriors fighting the hordes of the Lower Planes. To carry out this duty, he used this quill to create spell scrolls.
Crafted for the hand of a celestial being, the quill proves far more temperamental when wielded by a mortal. Its power is more limited, and the user cannot choose which spell will be inscribed upon the vellum.

STATISTICS:

Charge Ability:
- Three times per day, creates a random spell scroll.~

@40135 = ~Pipe of Devastation~
@40136 = ~This pipe once belonged to Balder Emberbreath, a wizard from the kingdom of Tethyr, renowned for his laid-back nature and love of peace and quiet. During his travels he befriended Narfi Braveheart, a stout halfling who introduced him to pipe-weed and gave him this very pipe as a gift.

Balder enjoyed smoking so much that, following the long-standing wizardly tradition of enchanting everyday objects, he imbued the pipe with powerful magic. With a simple command word, it can unleash devastating spells capable of swiftly eliminating anyone foolish enough to disturb his tranquility.

STATISTICS:

Charge Ability:

Three times per day, the pipe can create one of the following:
Cloud of Bestial Frenzy (each round, creatures inside must make a Save vs. Spell or become Berserk, gain +1 attack per round, and attack the nearest creature),
Incendiary Cloud, or
Deathmist (each round, creatures inside are attacked by 1d3 Phantom Assassins).~
@40137 = ~"Sprotch"~
@40138 = ~"Sprotch." What a strange name for a sling bullet...

The dubious honor for that name belongs to Kroc, a half-orc enchanter whose greatest passion in life was making things explode. Not the loud, flashy, city-leveling kind of explosions—no. His specialty was exploding living creatures into sticky, bloody messes.
As a child, he delighted in crushing insects. As he grew older, his victims simply became larger. Among his magical creations were the spells Corpse Explosion, Head Too Full Explosion, and Kroc's Delayed Explosion.
This sling bullet was his favorite creation.

STATISTICS:

Combat Abilities:

Returns to the wielder's sling after being fired.
30% chance to cause the target to explode instantly if it fails a Save vs. Death.

THAC0: +4
Damage: 2d4 +4
Damage Type: Missile (Piercing)
Weight: 0
Launcher: Sling~

@40139 = ~Shu Bhao's Belts~
@40140 = ~Shu Bhao remains one of the greatest warrior monks in the history of Shou Lung. Renowned for his courage and ferocity in battle, his mere presence enabled his people to win countless engagements.
He met his end when Emperor Kio Mong conquered T'ulung. Despite being his enemy, Mong held Bhao in the highest esteem and claimed his belts as trophies. They vanished after the emperor's death at the age of 108.

STATISTICS:

Equipped Abilities:
Improved Haste
Charge Ability:
Kai, 5 times per day~
